### Vertex shader deformation

I'll use `MeshPhysicalMaterial` + `onBeforeCompile` to inject:
- uniforms: `uPokes` (array of vec4: xyz = center in local space, w = amplitude), `uPokeDirs` (array of vec4: xyz = direction (inward normal at poke), w = radius), `uPokeBulge` maybe.
- Actually pack: `uniform vec4 uP0[N];` center+amp, `uniform vec4 uP1[N];` dir + radius.

Displacement function:
```glsl
vec3 pokeDisp(vec3 p){
  vec3 d = vec3(0.0);
  for (int i=0;i<N;i++){
    vec4 c = uP0[i]; float amp = c.w;
    if (abs(amp) < 1e-6) continue;
    vec3 dir = uP1[i].xyz; float R = uP1[i].w;
    float r = distance(p, c.xyz);
    float q = clamp(1.0 - (r*r)/(R*R), 0.0, 1.0);
    float g = q*q*(3.0-2.0*q); // smoothstep-ish, or q*q
    // inward displacement
    float inD = -amp * g;
    // bulge: outward radial perpendicular to dir
    vec3 rel = p - c.xyz;
    vec3 rad = rel - dir*dot(rel,dir);
    float rl = length(rad) ;
    vec3 radn = rl > 1e-5 ? rad/rl : vec3(0.0);
    float bg = q*(1.0-q)*4.0; // peaks at mid radius
    d += dir*inD + radn*amp*BULGE*bg;
  }
  return d;
}
```
Hmm, careful: the bulge should be proportional to the amount of tissue displaced inward. With amp>0 meaning "pushed in", the inward displacement is -amp*g along dir (dir = outward normal, so -dir*amp pushes in). Bulge outward radially by amp*BULGE*bg. Good.

